			<div class="content" id="post-content-2710877">
									

<p>I like the idea, but what throws the article off for me is a handfull of wonky or run on sentences.<br />
Some examples:</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;crit</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;crit</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>At some point in an approximately one month period immediately after an instance of SCP-2809-1’s parent or parents die,</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Shouldn't &quot;die&quot; be past tense here?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>If an instance of SCP-2809-1 clicks on an instance of SCP-2809-2 they are taken to the website of Quondam Holidays. Quondam Holidays is designated as SCP-2809-3.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I feel like this could be shortened into one sentence, as &quot;Quondam Holidays&quot; repeated twice sort of seems odd to me.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Investigation into Quondam Holidays is ongoing but little information has been discovered about them at this time apart from that gathered from interviews with SCP-2809-1 instances and their reviews of Quondam's service.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>A bit of a run on here. Maybe something to make it less wordy, such as</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Investigation into SCP-2809-3 is ongoing, with most information about them gathered from interviews of SCP-2809-1 instances and online reviews of SCP-2809-3.</p>
</blockquote>
</div>
