<p><span class="printuser"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/shaggydredlocks" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1722371); return false;">Shaggydredlocks</a></span>,</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">What's&nbsp;become&nbsp;rather&nbsp;a&nbsp;run-on&nbsp;response,&nbsp;sorry</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">Hide</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>I see exactly where you're coming from. The interview just isn't going to work for everyone. I'm sorry you're in that camp 'cause you've always given good feedback in the past but I'm leaving it as it is.</p>
<p>I was trying to show how the experience had helped the interviewee to process the death of a parent. She was still sad about it but had managed to come to terms with it in a way many people never do.</p>
<p>I put the &quot;do not seem to find anything unusual&quot; bit below the interview because I wanted a cause a little bit of cognitive dissonance in the reader while they were working through the interview.</p>
<p>I had hoped that the interview would provide a little sweet emotional impact with just a hint of bitterness for flavour but I guess it didn't do it for you. I'm sorry about that.</p>
<p>Still - I appreciate the feedback and I'm glad you liked the reviews. Thanks, person.</p>
</div>
